From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mcla-x86 SDK patch layer
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0211: integer cvars accept 0x hex and refuse trailing garbage

Source: $IOS games/mcla/sdk_patches/0211-int-cvar-hex-whole-numbers.patch (mcla-ios @ e24c5751), regenerated on the
  Renan series by W0.3, code unchanged (its own header kept below)
Licence: GPL-3 (owner's own work, part of mcla-x86); the patched files are ReXGlue SDK code (BSD-3-Clause)
Purpose: cvar: INT32/INT64/UINT32/UINT64 cvars read `0x180` as 384 and refuse `12abc` (command line, environment, TOML
  and SetFlagByName), instead of silently taking 0 or 12.
Bucket: AS-IS

0211-int-cvar-hex-whole-numbers.patch (our own, MCLA; E6 task, docs/mcla/LEARNINGS.md energy-e6)

Purpose. An integer cvar read "0x180" as 0 and "12abc" as 12, and the harness still printed `--set-at ... -> set`:
the setters of the REXCVAR_DEFINE_INT32 / INT64 / UINT32 / UINT64 macros (include/rex/cvar.h) call std::from_chars
and test only its error code, never where it stopped. A whole bench run of the E4 task (mcla_gfx_dl_disable=0x180)
tested nothing; the same silent 0 would hit a hex value in the launcher's Extra Args on the phone.

Change (src/core/cvar.cpp only, marked "LOCAL PATCH - MCLA (0211)": one translation unit, no header change, so no
rebuild of the users of cvar.h): NormalizeIntegerValue runs in ApplyFromSource (command line, environment, late
registrations) and SetFlagFromSource (SetFlagByName, the harness' --set-at, TOML config) before the value reaches
ValidateConstraints / the setter:
  * 0x<hex> (also -0x<hex> for the signed types) becomes its decimal text, so 0x180 = 384 and a hex value is range
    checked by the setter as before (0xFFFFFFFF into an INT32 is refused, not wrapped);
  * a decimal value that from_chars does not consume to the end ("12abc", "1.5", "5 ") is refused with
    `cvar 'NAME': 'TEXT' is not one whole number (decimal, or 0x hex): not set` (SetFlagByName returns false, the harness
    prints FAILED); a malformed hex value gets the "valid hexadecimal value" warning;
  * everything else (bool, double, string, a plain decimal) is untouched.
No effect on the guest. Applies after 0210 (its lines are in other files). Made by hand in
/Volumes/dev-ssd/mcla/scratch/energy-e6/sdk0211 (diff -u a b). prepare_sdk.py recognises "already applied" by reverse-checking.

Rebased for mcla-x86 (W0.3): regenerated with git diff on ReXGlue f5337cd + Renan v0.3.17 series; the original applies
too (git apply: hunk 2 at offset +508, after rexglue-cvar-toml-strings), code unchanged.

---
diff --git a/src/core/cvar.cpp b/src/core/cvar.cpp
index 18585bef..a71d8afc 100644
--- a/src/core/cvar.cpp
+++ b/src/core/cvar.cpp
@@ -100,9 +100,59 @@ bool Outranks(Source source, const FlagEntry& entry) {
   return source >= entry.source;
 }
 
+// LOCAL PATCH - MCLA (games/mcla/sdk_patches/0211): integer cvars take hexadecimal and refuse text that is not one whole number.
+// The setters of the INT32 / INT64 / UINT32 / UINT64 macros (cvar.h) call std::from_chars and look only at its error code, not at
+// where it stopped: "0x180" parsed as 0 (it stops at the 'x') and "12abc" as 12, and SetFlagByName / the command line / the
+// harness' --set-at still reported success (a whole bench run of the E4 task tested nothing, mcla_gfx_dl_disable=0x180 = 0). Every
+// path that applies a value (command line, environment, TOML config, SetFlagByName) calls this first: for an integer cvar the
+// value is one whole decimal integer, or 0x<hex> (a '-' before the 0x is allowed for the signed types), which is turned into its
+// decimal text; anything else is refused with a warning. A value out of range is still refused by the setter.
+bool NormalizeIntegerValue(const FlagEntry& entry, std::string_view& value, std::string& storage) {
+  const bool is_signed = entry.type == FlagType::Int32 || entry.type == FlagType::Int64;
+  if (!is_signed && entry.type != FlagType::Uint32 && entry.type != FlagType::Uint64) {
+    return true;
+  }
+  const char* begin = value.data();
+  const char* end = begin + value.size();
+  const char* p = begin;
+  bool negative = false;
+  if (p != end && *p == '-') {
+    negative = true;
+    ++p;
+  }
+  if (end - p > 2 && p[0] == '0' && (p[1] == 'x' || p[1] == 'X')) {
+    uint64_t magnitude = 0;
+    auto [ptr, ec] = std::from_chars(p + 2, end, magnitude, 16);
+    if (ec != std::errc() || ptr != end || (negative && !is_signed)) {
+      REXLOG_WARN("cvar '{}': '{}' is not a valid hexadecimal value for this type: not set", entry.name, value);
+      return false;
+    }
+    storage = (negative ? "-" : "") + std::to_string(magnitude);
+    value = storage;
+    return true;
+  }
+  if (is_signed) {
+    int64_t number = 0;
+    auto [ptr, ec] = std::from_chars(begin, end, number);
+    if (ec == std::errc() && ptr != end) {
+      REXLOG_WARN("cvar '{}': '{}' is not one whole number (decimal, or 0x hex): not set", entry.name, value);
+      return false;
+    }
+  } else {
+    uint64_t number = 0;
+    auto [ptr, ec] = std::from_chars(begin, end, number);
+    if (ec == std::errc() && ptr != end) {
+      REXLOG_WARN("cvar '{}': '{}' is not one whole number (decimal, or 0x hex): not set", entry.name, value);
+      return false;
+    }
+  }
+  return true;
+}
+
 // Unvalidated apply, for the command line and environment paths.
 bool ApplyFromSource(FlagEntry& entry, std::string_view value, Source source) {
-  if (!Outranks(source, entry) || !entry.setter(value)) {
+  std::string storage;  // LOCAL PATCH - MCLA (0211)
+  if (!Outranks(source, entry) || !NormalizeIntegerValue(entry, value, storage) || !entry.setter(value)) {
     return false;
   }
   entry.source = source;
@@ -832,6 +882,11 @@ ApplyResult SetFlagFromSource(std::string_view name, std::string_view value, Sou
     return ApplyResult::kRejected;
   }
 
+  std::string integer_text;  // LOCAL PATCH - MCLA (0211): hex -> decimal, trailing garbage refused
+  if (!NormalizeIntegerValue(entry, value, integer_text)) {
+    return ApplyResult::kRejected;
+  }
+
   if (!ValidateConstraints(entry, value)) {
     return ApplyResult::kRejected;
   }
